// ============ SECTION 2: THE CONSULTATION ============
h1("Section 2: The Consultation");
p("The consultation is where most of your revenue is won or lost. A patient who walks in curious should walk out with a plan they are excited about and a first treatment booked. This section gives the coordinator or consult-led provider a repeatable arc that feels like a conversation, not a pitch. Run it the same way every time and your conversion rate stops depending on who is in the room that day.");

h2("2.1  The Consultation Arc");
p("Every great consultation moves through the same five stages in order. Skipping a stage is the most common reason a consult does not close. The arc is connect, discover, reflect, recommend, and invite.");
bulletRich([{ text: "Connect. ", bold: true }, { text: "Build genuine rapport and lower the patient's guard." }]);
bulletRich([{ text: "Discover. ", bold: true }, { text: "Ask the questions that surface the real goal, the emotional driver, and the budget reality." }]);
bulletRich([{ text: "Reflect. ", bold: true }, { text: "Mirror back what you heard so the patient feels understood and gives you permission to lead." }]);
bulletRich([{ text: "Recommend. ", bold: true }, { text: "Present a clear plan tied to their goal, with the membership or package framed as the smart choice." }]);
bulletRich([{ text: "Invite. ", bold: true }, { text: "Assume the close, handle any hesitation with care, and book the first treatment before they leave." }]);

h2("2.2  Opening and Rapport");
p("The first two minutes decide whether the patient relaxes or stays guarded. Slow down, make it about them, and signal that this is a conversation rather than a sales appointment.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "It's so nice to meet you, [First Name]. Before we look at anything, I just want to get to know you a little. There's no script here and no pressure. My only job today is to understand what you're hoping for and tell you honestly what I'd do if I were in your shoes. Sound good?",
  "So tell me, what made today the day you decided to come in?"
]);
why("Naming the absence of pressure disarms the patient who arrived braced for a hard sell. The question about why today surfaces the emotional trigger, which is the most powerful piece of information you can have in the room.");

h2("2.3  Discovery: The Questions That Uncover The Real Goal");
p("Most patients describe a symptom, not the goal. They say they hate their forehead lines, but the goal is to stop looking tired and angry in meetings. Your job is to dig one level deeper to the feeling, because the feeling is what they will pay to change. Ask, then be quiet and let them talk.");
h3("The discovery questions");
microLabel("Ask, in order");
scriptBox([
  "When you look in the mirror, what's the first thing your eye goes to?",
  "How long has that been bothering you?",
  "When it bothers you most, what's happening? A photo, a meeting, getting ready to go out?",
  "If we fixed that, what would change for you day to day?",
  "Have you had anything done before, or would this be your first time?",
  "And just so I plan something realistic for you, were you thinking of this as a one-time thing, or are you open to a plan that gets you a real result and keeps you there?"
]);
why("The final question quietly qualifies budget and commitment without ever mentioning money, which lets you size your recommendation correctly rather than guessing. Asking what would change day to day pulls the patient into imagining the result, which is where desire is built.");

h2("2.4  The Reflect Moment");
p("Before you recommend anything, mirror back what you heard. This single step is the difference between a recommendation that feels like a prescription and one that feels like a pitch. When patients hear their own words repeated, they trust that the plan was built for them.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Okay, here's what I'm hearing, and tell me if I've got this right. The thing that bugs you most is [their words]. It's been on your mind for [time], and it really hits you when [their trigger]. What you actually want isn't to look done or different, you just want to look like a rested, refreshed version of you. Did I get that right?"
]);
why("Ending on a yes question earns a small agreement that builds momentum toward the larger yes. Reflecting the emotional outcome rather than the clinical symptom keeps the conversation anchored to what the patient will happily pay for.");

h2("2.5  Presenting the Treatment Plan");
p("Present the plan as one coherent path to their stated goal, not a menu of options. Patients freeze when handed choices and relax when handed a recommendation. Always present the full plan first. You can right-size later if you must, but you cannot un-ring the bell of a bigger vision.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "So here's what I'd do if you were my sister and you wanted [their goal]. We'd start with [treatment one] to handle [the main concern]. Then we'd add [treatment two] because that's what actually gets you that [outcome] you described, not just a quick fix. To really lock it in and keep it, we'd space that over [timeframe]. That's the plan that gets you there and keeps you there, and honestly it's what I'd want for myself."
]);
why("The if you were my sister framing signals that the recommendation is honest rather than self-interested, which is the trust patients are quietly testing for. Presenting the plan as a path that keeps them there naturally sets up the membership and package conversation that follows.");

h2("2.6  Presenting Price: Anchor, Then Offer The Smart Choice");
p("Present the full plan price first as the anchor, then reveal the membership or package as the way to get the same result for less. The sequence matters. A patient who hears the membership price after the full anchor experiences it as a saving, not an expense.");
microLabel("Step one: anchor the full plan");
scriptBox([
  "If we did this as individual treatments, the full plan would run about [$ full price]. That's the real number for everything we just talked about."
]);
microLabel("Step two: reveal the smart choice");
scriptBox([
  "Now here's what I'd actually recommend, because there's a smarter way to do this. If we set you up on [membership / package], you get the exact same plan, but it comes to [$ lower effective price]. You save [$ amount], you lock in member pricing on everything going forward, and you never have to think about it again. Same result, less money, and you're taken care of. That's the one I'd put you on."
]);
why("Anchoring high first makes the lower membership figure feel like relief rather than a new cost, which is the core mechanic of value-based selling. Stating plainly that this is the option you would choose removes the patient's burden of deciding and lets them simply agree.");

h2("2.7  The Assumptive Close");
p("Do not ask whether they want to proceed. Ask which detail to lock in. The assumptive close treats the yes as already decided and moves straight to logistics, which is where confident patients want to be.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "I love this plan for you. Let's get your first session booked while you're here so you get the timing you want. Are mornings or afternoons easier for you? And we can set the membership up today so this first visit counts toward it. Sound good?"
]);
why("Moving directly to scheduling logistics signals confidence and quietly carries the patient past the decision point. Offering to make the first visit count toward the membership rewards an immediate commitment and removes the feeling of paying twice.");

h2("2.8  Right-Sizing Without Discounting");
p("When the full plan is genuinely out of reach, never slash the price. Instead, reduce the scope while protecting the rate and the relationship. A right-sized plan that the patient actually completes is worth far more than a discounted full plan they resent.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Totally hear you, and I'd never want you stretching past what feels comfortable. Here's what we can do. Let's start with just [the highest-impact treatment], which is the piece that moves the needle most on [their goal]. That gets you [$ smaller number]. You'll see real results, and we can build from there whenever you're ready. The plan doesn't have to happen all at once, it just has to start."
]);
why("Protecting the rate while shrinking the scope preserves your pricing integrity and your margins. Framing the smaller start as building from there keeps the door open to the full plan over the lifetime of the relationship.");

rule();

// ============ SECTION 3: PROVIDERS ============
h1("Section 3: Providers (Nurse Practitioners, Injectors, RNs)");
p("Patients trust the person holding the needle more than anyone else in the building. That clinical authority is the most powerful conversion tool you have, and most providers leave it unused because they were trained to treat, not to recommend. A provider who simply does the treatment the patient booked is doing half the job. The other half is showing the patient the full path to the result they actually want.");

h2("3.1  The Provider's Role in Revenue");
p("Providers are not being asked to be salespeople. They are being asked to be honest about what it actually takes to get the result. When a patient books one syringe but needs two areas addressed to look balanced, saying so is good medicine and good business. Silence is the leak.");
bulletRich([{ text: "You are the authority. ", bold: true }, { text: "When you recommend something, it carries clinical weight nobody else's recommendation has. Use it generously and honestly." }]);
bulletRich([{ text: "Recommend the whole picture. ", bold: true }, { text: "Address what the patient sees, then point out what they will be glad you mentioned." }]);
bulletRich([{ text: "Book from the chair. ", bold: true }, { text: "The treatment seat is the highest-trust moment in the building. Pre-booking the next visit here converts far better than at the desk." }]);

h2("3.2  The In-Chair Consultation");
p("Even when a patient is in for a specific treatment, take sixty seconds to understand the bigger goal. This small habit turns a single-treatment patient into a treatment-plan patient.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Before I get started, let me take a real look at you in the mirror with you. You came in for [booked treatment], and we're absolutely doing that today. While I'm here though, I want to make sure I'm getting you the full result you're after, not just the one piece. When you look at yourself, what's the overall thing you want? To look more rested? Lifted? Refreshed?"
]);
why("Framing the look together at the mirror as collaborative invites the patient into a shared assessment rather than a sales pitch. Asking for the overall goal opens the door to recommending complementary treatments grounded in the patient's own words.");

h2("3.3  Recommending The Full Plan From The Chair");
p("Once you know the goal, recommend the complete approach with clinical confidence. Patients want their provider to have a point of view. Hedging reads as uncertainty and costs you the recommendation.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Okay, so here's my honest read as your provider. What we're doing today is going to soften [the area]. But the thing that's actually making you look tired is [secondary area], and if we only treat one and not the other, you won't get the balanced result you're describing. My recommendation is that we plan to address both, ideally over [timeframe]. I'd rather tell you the truth and let you decide than have you wonder later why you didn't get the full result."
]);
why("Leading with my honest read as your provider activates the clinical trust that only the provider holds. Explaining why a partial treatment underdelivers gives the patient a concrete, non-pushy reason to commit to the full plan.");

h2("3.4  Cross-Selling Complementary Treatments");
p("The best cross-sell is the one that makes the current treatment work better. Tie every additional recommendation to the result the patient already wants, never to a sales target.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "One more thing while you're here, and take it or leave it. The [current treatment] is going to do its job, but the reason your skin still looks a little tired is texture and tone, which this won't touch. A [complementary treatment, for example a medical facial or a resurfacing series] is what actually gives you that lit-from-within look on top of what we did today. A lot of my patients pair them, and they're always glad they did. Want me to have the front desk show you how that'd work together?"
]);
why("Positioning the add-on as the missing piece of a result the patient already paid for makes it feel like completing the picture rather than buying more. The soft handoff to the desk keeps the provider in the trusted-advisor role rather than the closer role.");

h2("3.5  Pre-Booking The Next Session From The Chair");
p("The most powerful rebooking moment in the building is while the patient is still in your chair, results fresh and trust at its peak. Set the next appointment as a clinical necessity, not an option.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Here's what's going to happen. This'll settle beautifully over the next two weeks, and then around [X weeks] is exactly when we want to see you again to keep this result going. If we wait too long, you lose ground and we're starting over, which is a waste of your money. So before you even get up, let's make sure the front desk locks in [X weeks] from today for you. I'll walk you out and tell them myself."
]);
why("Framing the next visit as the point where results would otherwise be lost ties rebooking to protecting the investment the patient just made. The provider personally handing the patient to the desk transfers clinical authority directly into a booked appointment.");

h2("3.6  Introducing Membership From The Clinical Seat");
p("When the membership comes from the provider, it lands as clinical guidance rather than a sales offer. Keep it brief and tie it to consistency of results.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Real quick, and then I'll let you relax. The patients who get the best results from what we just did are the ones who stay consistent, and our membership is built exactly for that. It keeps you on schedule, it saves you money on every visit, and honestly it's what I'd want my own family on. I'll have the front desk set it up so today counts toward it. You'll thank yourself in six months."
]);
why("A membership recommended by the provider inherits clinical credibility that a front-desk offer cannot match. Connecting membership to consistency of results frames it as part of the treatment plan rather than an upsell.");

rule();

// ============ SECTION 4: ESTHETICIANS ============
h1("Section 4: Estheticians");
p("Estheticians have the longest hands-on time with a patient of anyone in the building, often forty-five minutes of quiet trust. That time is a gift and a responsibility. A skilled esthetician turns one facial into a series, a series into a membership, and a skincare-only patient into a future injectables patient through a warm, credible handoff. This section gives estheticians language that recommends without ever feeling like selling.");

h2("4.1  The Esthetician's Revenue Role");
bulletRich([{ text: "You build the skin foundation. ", bold: true }, { text: "Every result the providers deliver looks better on healthy, prepped skin, and you are the one who creates it. Say so." }]);
bulletRich([{ text: "Series beat one-offs. ", bold: true }, { text: "Real skin change takes a course of treatments. Recommending a series is honest, because a single facial rarely delivers what the patient wants." }]);
bulletRich([{ text: "You are the gateway. ", bold: true }, { text: "A patient who trusts you with their skin will trust your referral to a provider. The warm handoff is one of the highest-converting moments in the spa." }]);

h2("4.2  The Skincare Consultation During The Facial");
p("Use the quiet of the treatment to understand the patient's skin goals and history. Listen far more than you talk, and take mental notes for your recommendation at the end.");
microLabel("Say (early in the treatment)");
scriptBox([
  "While I work, I'd love to hear what's going on with your skin. What's frustrating you most right now? And what does your routine look like at home, honestly, no judgment? The more I know, the better I can tell you what's actually going to move the needle for you versus what's a waste of money."
]);
why("Inviting an honest account of their routine without judgment surfaces the gaps you will later fill with a recommendation. Promising to separate what works from what wastes money positions you as a trusted guide rather than a retail seller.");

h2("4.3  Recommending a Treatment Series");
p("At the end of the facial, when the patient is glowing and relaxed, connect today's result to the series that sustains it. Be specific about the number and the spacing so it feels like a plan rather than a vague suggestion.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "So here's the honest truth about your skin. Today gave you a beautiful reset, and you're going to glow for a few days. But what you described wanting, that clear, even, lit-from-within skin, that's not a one-facial thing. It comes from a series. For where your skin is, I'd want to see you every [X weeks] for [number] treatments, and that's when you actually see the change hold. The good news is we can package those so it costs you less than booking them one at a time. Want me to set that up with the front desk?"
]);
why("Telling the patient plainly that one facial cannot deliver their goal is both honest and the strongest possible case for a series. Mentioning that a package costs less than separate bookings introduces the value framing without sounding like a discount pitch.");

h2("4.4  Homecare and Retail Without Feeling Salesy");
p("Recommend products as the homework that protects the in-spa investment, never as a sales add-on. Frame retail as the difference between results that last and results that fade.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "I'm going to be straight with you because I want you to actually get results. What you do at home matters more than what I do here once a month. Right now there are two things missing from your routine that are holding your skin back. I'm not going to send you home with ten products you won't use. Just these two, this one and this one, and they're the ones that make everything we did today actually last. Can I set those aside for you?"
]);
why("Promising not to push ten products and recommending only two builds credibility that makes the patient far more likely to buy. Framing homecare as what makes the treatment last ties retail to the result rather than to a transaction.");

h2("4.5  The Warm Handoff To A Provider");
p("When you notice a concern outside your scope, refer to the provider with genuine enthusiasm. This handoff converts because it carries your trust with it. Never make it feel like a deflection.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Can I tell you something I noticed? Your skin is in a great place for facials, and we'll keep that going. But that thing you mentioned about looking tired around the eyes, that's actually not a skincare fix, that's where one of our providers can do something I can't. I'd love to introduce you to [Provider]. They're incredible, and a quick consult is free. Want me to walk you over and introduce you before you go?"
]);
why("Distinguishing clearly between what skincare can and cannot fix makes the referral feel like honest guidance rather than a pass-off. A personal introduction transfers the patient's trust in you directly to the provider and dramatically raises the odds they book.");

h2("4.6  Membership For The Skincare Patient");
p("The facial patient is one of the easiest members to enroll because they already plan to come back monthly. Connect the membership to the rhythm their skin needs.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Since your skin really does best on a monthly rhythm, our membership is kind of made for you. It's [$ a month], it covers your monthly treatment, and you get member pricing on all your homecare too. Most of my regulars are on it because it saves them real money and keeps them consistent, which is the whole secret to good skin. Want me to have the front desk get you set up so today counts toward it?"
]);
why("Connecting membership to the monthly rhythm the skin needs makes enrollment feel like following the treatment plan rather than signing up for a program. Noting that consistency is the secret to good skin gives a credible, results-based reason to commit.");

rule();

