]);
why("Stating that there is no shortcut to the result establishes the package as a clinical reality rather than an upsell. Contrasting the package with the expensive way of booking one at a time makes committing to the full course feel like the financially smart decision.");

h2("6.3  Financing And Payment Language");
p("Price resistance is often a cash-flow concern, not a value concern. Offering a payment path can turn a no into a yes without ever touching your price. Present financing as a convenience, calmly and without embarrassment.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "And if it's easier to spread this out, we've got you. A lot of our patients use [financing option] and break it into manageable monthly payments instead of all at once. It doesn't change the price at all, it just makes it comfortable. Want me to show you what that'd look like per month? Sometimes seeing it that way makes the decision a lot easier."
]);
why("Presenting financing as something many patients use normalizes it and removes any stigma the patient might feel. Reframing a large total as a comfortable monthly figure addresses the real barrier, which is usually cash flow rather than the value of the result.");

h2("6.4  Package Objection Handling");
h3("That is a lot of money up front");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "I hear you, it's a real investment. Here's the way I'd think about it though. Booked separately, the same treatments would actually cost you more, you'd just be paying it in pieces and probably not finishing. The package saves you money and makes sure you get the result. And if up front is the issue, we can break it into payments. Would monthly make this easier for you?"
]);
h3("What if it does not work");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Great question, and I'd want to know that too. Here's the honest answer. A course of treatments is exactly how we make sure it works, because one session was never going to get you there. This protocol is what gets results for patients with [their concern]. We'll track your progress at every visit, and I'll be honest with you the whole way. You're not guessing, you're following a plan that works."
]);
why("Acknowledging the size of the investment before reframing it keeps the patient from feeling dismissed. Tying the package directly to the certainty of the result answers the unspoken fear behind every high-ticket objection, which is the fear of wasting money.");

rule();

// ============ SECTION 7: EXISTING PATIENT EXPANSION ============
h1("Section 7: Existing Patient Expansion");
p("Your existing patients are your most overlooked growth engine. They already trust you, they already buy, and they cost nothing to acquire. Yet most med spas treat every visit as a repeat of the last one rather than a chance to deepen the relationship. This section gives your team the language to expand existing patients into new services, memberships, larger packages, and referrals, all without ever feeling pushy with someone who already knows you.");

h2("7.1  The Expansion Mindset");
p("Expansion is not about extracting more from loyal patients. It is about making sure they know everything you can do for them. Many patients stay narrow simply because no one ever told them about the treatment that would change their life. Silence is not respect, it is a disservice.");

h2("7.2  Cross-Sell To An Existing Patient");
p("Anchor the new recommendation in the trust you have already earned and the results they have already seen. Connect the new service to a goal they have mentioned before.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "You've been one of my favorite patients to work with, and your [existing treatment] results are gorgeous. Because I know your skin so well now, I've actually been wanting to mention something. The next thing that would take you to a whole other level is [new treatment]. It targets [the thing they've mentioned wanting], which is the one piece we haven't touched yet. I really think you'd love the result. Can I tell you how it'd work for you?"
]);
why("Referencing the long relationship and the results they have already seen makes the new recommendation feel earned rather than opportunistic. Framing the new service as the one piece we have not touched yet presents expansion as completing their journey.");

h2("7.3  Upgrading A Pay-As-You-Go Patient To Membership");
p("Loyal non-members are leaving savings on the table and you are leaving predictable revenue on the table. Approach the conversation as doing them a favor you should have done sooner.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Can I be honest about something? You come in regularly, you're basically already a member in everything but name, and you've been paying full price this whole time. I actually feel a little bad I haven't set you up sooner. If we put you on the membership, you'd get member pricing on everything you already do and you'd save real money this year. Let me fix that for you today. Sound good?"
]);
why("Framing the missed membership as something you feel bad about removes any sense of a sales motive and positions enrollment as correcting an oversight in the patient's favor. Patients who already buy regularly find this almost impossible to refuse because the savings are self-evident.");

h2("7.4  Upgrading To A VIP Or Annual Tier");
p("Your best patients often want to be recognized as your best patients. An annual or VIP tier rewards them while locking in a year of revenue. Present it as an invitation, not a sale.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "I want to let you in on something we don't offer to everyone. Because you're one of our most loyal patients, you'd be perfect for our [VIP / annual] tier. It's our best pricing, you get [premium perks], and you're first in line for everything. It's really our way of taking care of the patients who take care of us. Want me to tell you what's included?"
]);
why("Positioning the upgrade as something not offered to everyone makes it feel like recognition rather than an upsell, which appeals to a loyal patient's desire to be valued. Inviting them into a tier reserved for the best patients turns a transaction into a status they want to claim.");

h2("7.5  The Referral Ask");
p("Happy patients refer when you make it easy and give them a reason. The best time to ask is right after a great result, when their enthusiasm is highest. Be direct and warm, never apologetic.");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Okay, you look incredible, and I have to ask you a favor. The best compliment you can give me is sending me someone you love. If you've got a friend who's been wanting to do something for themselves, send them my way. I'll take amazing care of them, and I'll make sure you both get [referral perk] as a thank you. Who comes to mind?"
]);
why("Asking who comes to mind rather than whether they know anyone prompts the patient to actually picture specific people, which produces far more referrals. Tying the ask to a great result and a mutual reward makes referring feel generous to their friend rather than like doing the spa a favor.");

