const B = require("./build.js");
const {
  C, h1, h2, h3, p, pr, bullet, bulletRich, num, spacer, microLabel,
  scriptBox, why, rule,
  INK, ACCENT, ACCENT_DK, BLUSH, RULE, GREY, CW,
  Document, Packer, Paragraph, TextRun, Table, TableRow, TableCell,
  AlignmentType, LevelFormat, TableOfContents, HeadingLevel, BorderStyle,
  WidthType, ShadingType, PageNumber, Header, Footer, fs
} = B;

// ============ SECTION 9: OBJECTION LIBRARY ============
h1("Section 9: Universal Objection Handling Library");
p("Objections are not rejections. They are requests for more information or reassurance, and a patient who objects is usually still interested. Every objection in aesthetics traces back to one of three fears: it costs too much, it will not work, or I am not ready. The structure for handling all of them is the same: acknowledge the concern sincerely, reframe it, then guide the patient gently back toward the decision. Never argue. Agree, then lead.");

h3("The acknowledge, reframe, guide structure");
p("Whatever the objection, your team should run it through the same three beats. Acknowledge so the patient feels heard. Reframe so the concern looks different. Guide with a question that moves toward yes.");

h2("9.1  It is too expensive");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "I completely understand, and I'd never want you stretching past what's comfortable. Can I reframe it though? It feels like a big number because we're looking at the whole result at once. Broken down, this is [$ per month or per visit], and it's the thing that actually gets you [their goal] instead of spending smaller amounts on stuff that doesn't quite work. Would it feel better to spread it out, or to start with the highest-impact piece first?"
]);

h2("9.2  I need to think about it");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Totally fair, this should never feel rushed. Can I ask, just so I understand, is it the timing, the investment, or are you not totally sure it's right for you? Because whichever one it is, I can probably help you think it through right now. What's the piece you're sitting with?"
]);
why("Asking which of the three concerns is actually behind the delay surfaces the real objection, because I need to think about it is almost always a polite stand-in for a specific worry. Once the real concern is named, it can be answered directly rather than left to harden overnight.");

h2("9.3  I need to talk to my partner");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Of course, that makes total sense. Let me make that conversation easy for you. Here's exactly what we talked about and the investment, written down so you have it. And honestly, most partners just want to know two things: that it matters to you and that it's a smart decision. On both counts, you're covered. Should we pencil in your first session now so you have the spot, knowing you can move it after you chat?"
]);
why("Giving the patient a clear written summary equips them to advocate for the purchase rather than relay it vaguely, which is where partner conversations usually fall apart. Holding the spot while leaving room to confirm keeps momentum without applying pressure.");

h2("9.4  Let me check my schedule");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "Smart, let's look at it together right now so you don't lose the spot you want. I've got [day] at [time] or [day] at [time]. Pull up your calendar and let's just see which one's clean. Worst case we move it later, but at least you're holding your ideal time."
]);

h2("9.5  I saw it cheaper somewhere else");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "I'm really glad you brought that up, because price is the easy part to compare and the dangerous part to choose on. With injectables and devices, you're not buying a product, you're buying the hands and the judgment of the person doing it. Cheaper usually means less experience, less product, or a result that doesn't last. I'd rather you pay once and love it than pay twice fixing it. Can I show you why our patients happily pay for the result they get here?"
]);
why("Reframing the purchase as buying the provider's skill rather than a commodity makes a lower price elsewhere read as a risk rather than a bargain. The phrase pay once and love it crystallizes the real cost of choosing on price alone.");

h2("9.6  I am not ready");
microLabel("Say");
scriptBox([
  "That's completely okay, and there's no pressure at all. Can I ask what ready looks like for you? Sometimes people are waiting for a sign that never quite comes, and the truth is the best time to start something like this was a while ago, and the second best time is now. What would need to be true for you to feel ready?"
]);

h2("9.7  Silence after the price");
p("Silence is not a no. It is the patient processing. The hardest and most important skill in sales is to stay quiet and let them think. Resist the urge to fill the gap with a discount.");
microLabel("Do this");
scriptBox([
  "Say the price, then stop talking. Hold a warm, comfortable silence and let the patient respond first. The next person to speak after the price is usually the one who concedes. Smile, stay relaxed, and wait."
]);
why("Filling the silence almost always means lowering the price or talking the patient out of the sale, which trains them to expect discounts. A confident pause communicates that the price is fair and final, which is exactly what a hesitating patient needs to feel.");

rule();

// ============ SECTION 10: MULTI-TOUCH FOLLOW-UP ============
h1("Section 10: The Multi-Touch Follow-Up Sequence");
p("Most consultations that do not close on the spot are not lost, they are abandoned. The patient leaves genuinely interested, life gets busy, and no one follows up. A structured follow-up sequence recovers a meaningful share of these patients and is one of the highest-return habits a med spa can build. The rule is simple: a consultation that did not book a treatment triggers a sequence automatically, every time, with no exceptions.");

h2("10.1  The Five-Touch Sequence For A Consult That Did Not Close");
p("Run this cadence for any patient who left without booking. Each touch is warm, adds value, and gently keeps the door open. Stop the sequence the moment they book or ask to be left alone.");

h3("Touch 1: Same day, a few hours after the consult (text)");
microLabel("Text");
scriptBox([
  "[First Name], it was so lovely meeting you today. I meant what I said, I really think we can get you that [their goal]. Whenever you're ready, your plan is here waiting and so am I. No rush at all."
]);

h3("Touch 2: Day 2 (email with the written plan)");
microLabel("Email");
scriptBox([
  "Hi [First Name], I put your plan in writing so you have everything in one place. Here's what we talked through and the investment for each piece. The whole thing is built around the [their goal] you told me about. Take your time, and reply here with any question at all. I'm happy to adjust anything to fit you."
]);

h3("Touch 3: Day 5 (text, value and reassurance)");
microLabel("Text");
scriptBox([
  "Hi [First Name], thinking of you. A lot of patients tell me they wish they'd started sooner once they see the results. If anything's holding you back, even a small thing, tell me and let's figure it out together. No pressure ever."
]);

h3("Touch 4: Day 10 (gentle reason to act)");
microLabel("Text");
scriptBox([
  "Hey [First Name], I've got a couple of openings come up with [Provider] over the next two weeks and I thought of you. Want me to hold one so you have it? You can always move it. Just say the word."
]);

h3("Touch 5: Day 21 (the warm close-out)");
microLabel("Text");
scriptBox([
  "[First Name], I don't want to crowd your inbox, so this is my last note for now. Just know your plan is always here and I'd love to take care of you whenever the timing's right. Reply anytime and I'll get you in. Take care of yourself."
]);
why("Spacing the touches and varying them between reassurance, value, and a soft reason to act keeps the sequence from feeling like nagging. The warm close-out on touch five often reactivates patients precisely because it removes pressure and signals genuine care rather than a sales chase.");

h2("10.2  Integrating AI And Automated Follow-Up");
p("This sequence should be loaded into your CRM and triggered automatically the moment a consultation is marked unbooked, so no patient ever slips through because a team member forgot. Automation handles the timing and the first draft, while a human adds the personal detail, the patient's actual goal in their own words, before each message sends. The combination of automated reliability and human warmth is what makes the sequence convert. Personalized automation is one of the core systems installed during a Luxora Growth Engine engagement, alongside the CRM, reputation, and lifetime value frameworks that keep these conversations running without depending on memory or motivation.");

rule();

// ============ SECTION 11: IMPLEMENTATION ============
h1("Section 11: Putting This Playbook To Work");
p("Scripts on a page change nothing. Scripts practiced out loud, tracked, and coached change everything. This section turns the playbook from a document into a habit. The spas that win with this material treat it as a living system, not a one-time read.");

h2("11.1  The Roll-Out Plan");
num("Assign each role its section. Every team member reads and owns the sections that apply to them within the first week.", "rollnum");
num("Run weekly role-play. Pair team members and practice one scenario out loud each week until the language feels natural. Reading is not the same as rehearsing.", "rollnum");
num("Record and review real interactions. With consent, review actual calls and consults against these scripts to spot where the arc breaks down.", "rollnum");
num("Coach the weak point, not the whole script. Identify the single moment where conversions drop, usually the price reveal or the rebooking ask, and drill that one beat.", "rollnum");
num("Make rebooking and membership non-negotiable. These two questions get asked at every relevant touchpoint, every time, until they are automatic.", "rollnum");

h2("11.2  Track Conversion By Role");
p("What gets measured gets improved. Track these numbers by individual team member so coaching is specific rather than general.");
bulletRich([{ text: "Front desk: ", bold: true }, { text: "inquiry-to-booked-consult rate, confirmation-to-show rate, and check-out rebooking rate." }]);
bulletRich([{ text: "Consult and providers: ", bold: true }, { text: "consult-to-treatment-plan close rate, average plan value, and membership enrollment rate." }]);
bulletRich([{ text: "Estheticians: ", bold: true }, { text: "facial-to-series conversion, retail attachment rate, and provider-handoff bookings." }]);
bulletRich([{ text: "Whole spa: ", bold: true }, { text: "membership penetration, average patient lifetime value, and reactivation rate from the lapsed list." }]);

h2("11.3  The Standard Every Team Member Holds");
p("If your team remembers nothing else, they remember this. Diagnose before you prescribe. Lead with the outcome, not the product. Always present the smart financial choice. And never let a patient leave without the next step booked. Hold those four standards at every touchpoint and the revenue takes care of itself.");

rule();

// ============ CLOSING: TWO WEAKEST PARTS ============
h1("A Note On The Two Weakest Parts Of This Playbook");
p("Per the Luxora standard, here are the two parts of this deliverable that are the weakest as written, along with the stronger alternative for each. Both depend on details specific to your client's spa that should be filled in before the playbook is handed to a team.");

h3("Weakness one: every price and savings figure is a placeholder");
p("The scripts use bracketed placeholders such as the full price, the lower effective price, and the amount saved. Generic placeholders are the single most common reason a sales script fails in practice, because a team member who reaches a bracket mid-conversation hesitates, and hesitation kills the close. The stronger alternative is to build a one-page pricing companion for each client that fills in every bracket with their real numbers: actual membership price, actual package savings, actual financing monthly figures. The playbook teaches the language, the companion sheet makes it deployable on day one. Recommend producing that companion sheet as the immediate next deliverable for any client receiving this playbook.");

h3("Weakness two: the consultation arc assumes a confident closer");
p("Section 2 presents the close as a smooth, linear arc, which works beautifully for a naturally confident coordinator and falls apart for a clinical team member who freezes at the price reveal. The playbook as written under-serves the nervous closer, who is the exact person most spas actually have at the desk. The stronger alternative is to add a short companion module called the Reluctant Closer's Toolkit: three or four micro-scripts for the specific moments that intimidate non-salespeople, namely saying a high number out loud, sitting in silence after the price, and asking for the booking. Pairing the full arc with a confidence toolkit for the people who find selling uncomfortable would meaningfully lift real-world conversion, because the bottleneck in most spas is not the script, it is the comfort of the person delivering it.");

